#{[17592186345342 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -7 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -8 & -9 & 4\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$"] [17592186345342 :question/worked-solution "Using a cofactor expansion along the $2$nd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -7 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -8 & -9 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\cdot {C}_{12}+(-7)\\cdot {C}_{22}+0\\cdot {C}_{32}+(-8)\\cdot {C}_{42} \\\\  & =(-7)\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -9 & 4\\end{vmatrix}+(-8)\\cdot (-1)^{4+2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-7\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -9 & 4\\end{vmatrix}-8\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186348308 :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as:\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)){(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}^{′}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)){(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}^{′} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =2({cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =2(1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186348309 :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as:\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =({x}^{2}){({x}^{3})}^{′}-({x}^{3}){({x}^{2})}^{′} \\\\  & =({x}^{2})(3{x}^{2})-({x}^{3})(2x) \\\\  & =3{x}^{4}-2{x}^{4} \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186348310 :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as:\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${y}_{1}(x)=2x+1$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=x-1.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =(2x+1){(x-1)}^{′}-(x-1){(2x+1)}^{′} \\\\  & =(2x+1)(1)-(x-1)(2) \\\\  & =2x+1-2x+2 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186348312 :question/worked-solution "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(t)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(t) & =-{e}^{-t} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t}+2(-{e}^{-t})+{e}^{-t} & =(1-2+1){e}^{-t}=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement “${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-t}$ is a solution to the equation” is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(t) & =-{e}^{1-t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{1-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{1-t}+2(-{e}^{1-t})+{e}^{1-t} & =(1-2+1){e}^{1-t}=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{1-t}$ is a solution to the equation” is true.\n\n- So, both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian.\n\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & ={e}^{-t}{({e}^{1-t})}^{′}-{({e}^{-t})}^{′}{e}^{1-t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}(-{e}^{1-t})-(-{e}^{-t}){e}^{1-t} \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-2t}+{e}^{1-2t} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0,$ the functions are not linearly independent, hence $y=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{1-t}$  is not the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement “The general solution of the equation is $y=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{1-t}.$” is false."] [17592186351408 :question/problem "Which of the following determinants are equal to $0?$\n\n$$\n|A|=\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,|B|=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix},\\,|C|=\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186351409 :question/problem "Which of the following determinants are equal to $0?$\n\n$$\n|A|=\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix},\\,|B|=\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 2 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -8\\end{vmatrix},\\,|C|=\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{5} & -2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186351500 :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's Formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2} &=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} &=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the product of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the determinant of $A.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} & =det(A) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot 7-(-5)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-28+15 \\\\  & =-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186351501 :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's Formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2} &=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} &=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, the product of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the determinant of $A.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} & =det(A) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-5)-4\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =10-24 \\\\  & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186351506 :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(1,0,0)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨1,0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨{e}^{t},\\,{t}^{3},\\,2t⟩ & =⟨1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} &=1 \\\\ {t}^{3} &=0 \\\\ 2t &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=0.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,0,0)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$  and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,3{t}^{2},\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨1,0,2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,6t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =2j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(\\sqrt{5})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186351507 :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(2,1,0)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨2,1,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩ & =⟨2,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2t &=2 \\\\ {t}^{2} &=1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡t &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(2,1,0)$ is at the tip of $r(1).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(1),$  and $‖{r}^{′}(1)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2t,\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨2,2,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2,\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(1) & =⟨0,2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1) & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =-4i+2j+4k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+4+16} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(1) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(1){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{3}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186351508 :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by:\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+2{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the second derivative ${r}^{″}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\times ⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}i-\\sqrt{2}j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the magnitude of the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+{(-\\sqrt{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2+2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{2}{(\\sqrt{2})^{3}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186354308 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$\n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 0 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 4-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ -7 & 0 & -5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ -7 & -5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )(-5-\\lambda )+21) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +11) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the quadratic polynomial ${\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +11=0,$ we have that the discriminant $D<0.$ So, it doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable over $R.$"] [17592186354384 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{pmatrix}12 \\\\ 3\\end{pmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186354385 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{pmatrix}36 \\\\ 33\\end{pmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186354441 :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's Formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2} &=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} &=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, the product of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the determinant of $A.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} & =det(A) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2\\sqrt{2})\\cdot 2-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =-7\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186354442 :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's Formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2} &=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} &=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the product of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the determinant of $A.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} & =det(A) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-3)-\\sqrt{5}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =-15-10 \\\\  & =-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186354446 :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}-7A+8I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}-7A+8I) & =O \\\\ A-7I+8{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{7}{8}I-\\frac{1}{8}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{7}{8},$ $q=-\\frac{1}{8}$ and\n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{7}{8}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$"] [17592186354447 :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}5-\\lambda  & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}5-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((5-\\lambda )^{2}+4) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +29) & =0 \\\\ -{\\lambda }^{3}+12{\\lambda }^{2}-49\\lambda +58 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{A}^{3}+12{A}^{2}-49A+58I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}(-{A}^{3}+12{A}^{2}-49A+58I) & =O \\\\ -{A}^{2}+12A-49I+58{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{58}(49I-12A+{A}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186354509 :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})⟩=⟨0,\\,3,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(3)^{2}+(3{t}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+9{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9(1+{t}^{4})} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the second derivative ${r}^{″}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,6t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the cross-product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨0,\\,3,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,0,\\,6t⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3{t}^{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 3{t}^{2} \\\\ 0 & 6t\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3{t}^{2} \\\\ 0 & 6t\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =18t\\,i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the magnitude of the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =|18t|=18t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $t>0$, we have $|t|=t.$\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{18t}{{(3\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}})}^{3}}=\\frac{2t}{3(1+{t}^{4})^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186354511 :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩=⟨-1,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the second derivative ${r}^{″}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the cross-product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨-1,0,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\times ⟨0,0,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the magnitude of the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =|\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\ge 0$ when $0\\le t\\le \\pi .$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡(t))^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186354512 :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{3})⟩=⟨3,-3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-3{t}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+9{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3{t}^{2})⟩=⟨0,-6t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨3,\\,-3{t}^{2},\\,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,-6t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -3{t}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6t & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3{t}^{2} \\\\ 0 & -6t\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =-18t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖=|-18t|=18t.\n$$\n\nNotice that since $t>0$, we have $|t|=t.$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18t}{(3\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18t}{27(1+{t}^{4})^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{3(1+{t}^{4})^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186354636 :question/problem "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=4,\n$$\nfind \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}a+1 & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186354636 :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a+1 & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}a+1 & b+0 & c+0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $1$st row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a+1 & b+0 & c+0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\underbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}}_{4}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4+1(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186354637 :question/problem "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}a+2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b+3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=5,\n$$\nfind \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}a+1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186354637 :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a+1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}(a+2)-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ (b+3)-3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ c+0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $3$rd row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}(a+2)-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ (b+3)-3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ c+0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\underbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}(a+2) & -2 & 2 \\\\ (b+3) & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}}_{5}+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5+1\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5+(-10) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186354639 :question/problem "Find\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 91 & 92 & 93 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186354639 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $2$nd row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 91 & 92 & 93 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 90+1 & 92+0 & 93+0 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 90+1 & 92+0 & 93+0 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $2$nd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{vmatrix} & =0+1(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 92 & 93\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(3\\cdot 93-6\\cdot 92) \\\\  & =-(279-552) \\\\  & =273\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186354640 :question/problem "Find\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}97 & 98 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186354640 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}97 & 98 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}96+1 & 98+0 & 99+0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}96+1 & 98+0 & 99+0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}96 & 98 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $1$st row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}96 & 98 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{vmatrix} & =0+1(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 98 & 99\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 99-2\\cdot 98 \\\\  & =396-196 \\\\  & =200\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186354663 :answer/value "2\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 5\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186354687 :answer/value "2\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 & 13 \\\\ 11 & 9 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 16\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186354711 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186354713 :answer/value "-12\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -7 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186354735 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 12 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -6\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186354739 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -10 & 1 & 10 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 5\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186355002 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 10 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 10\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186355069 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186355127 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 10\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186355228 :answer/value "2\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 6\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186355250 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186355313 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}14 & -20 & -21 \\\\ 17 & -31 & -23 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 6\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186355476 :answer/value "2\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 & 13 \\\\ 11 & 9 & 26 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 10\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186355574 :answer/value "-3\\begin{vmatrix}-11 & 4 & 10 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -5\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186355612 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186355700 :answer/value "-3\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -4 & 12 \\\\ -7 & 2 & -6\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186355749 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}14 & -20 & -21 \\\\ 17 & -31 & -2 \\\\ 10 & 5 & -3\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186355752 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186355906 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186356087 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 10 & -10 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 13\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186356109 :answer/value "-3\\begin{vmatrix}-11 & 4 & 10 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -3\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186356162 :answer/value "2\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 5\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186356203 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186356353 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 7 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186356537 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -3\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186357466 :question/worked-solution "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+16(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement “${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x$ is a solution to the equation” is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+16(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement “${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x$ is a solution to the equation” is true.\n\n- So, both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian.\n\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)}^{′}-{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)-(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\  & =4({cos}^{2}⁡4x+{sin}^{2}⁡4x) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not equal to $0,$ the functions are linearly independent, hence $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x$  is the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement “The general solution of the equation is $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.$” is true."] [17592186357503 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 8 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries above and below ${a}_{23}$ equal to zero.*"] [17592186357503 :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries above and below ${a}_{23}=-2$ equal to zero using elementary row operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 8 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 & 0 & 13 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 11 & 9 & 0 & 26 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 0 & 10\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $3$rd column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 & 0 & 13 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 11 & 9 & 0 & 26 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 0 & 10\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-1)^{2+3}\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 & 13 \\\\ 11 & 9 & 26 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 10\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 & 13 \\\\ 11 & 9 & 26 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 10\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186357508 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -1 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{43}$ equal to zero.*"] [17592186357508 :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{43}=-1$ equal to zero using elementary column operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+2{C}_{3} \\\\ {C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+(-4){C}_{3} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+(-3){C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}14 & -20 & 6 & -21 \\\\ 17 & -31 & 7 & -23 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $4$th row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}14 & -20 & 6 & -21 \\\\ 17 & -31 & 7 & -23 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{4+3}\\begin{vmatrix}14 & -20 & -21 \\\\ 17 & -31 & -23 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}14 & -20 & -21 \\\\ 17 & -31 & -23 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 6\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186357512 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{22}$ equal to zero.*"] [17592186357512 :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{22}=1$ equal to zero using elementary column operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+2{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+3{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+(-4){C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 10 & -10 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -4 & 13\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $2$nd row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 10 & -10 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -4 & 13\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 10 & -10 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 13\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 10 & -10 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 13\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186357513 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left of ${a}_{44}$ equal to zero.*"] [17592186357513 :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries to the left of ${a}_{44}=1$ equal to zero using elementary column operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+(-4){C}_{4} \\\\ {C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+2{C}_{4} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+(-3){C}_{4}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -10 & 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 10 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $4$th row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -10 & 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 10 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 10\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 10\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186357514 :question/problem "Calculate the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the right of ${a}_{21}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*"] [17592186357514 :question/worked-solution "Using elementary column operations, let's make entries to the right of ${a}_{21}=1$ equal to zero.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+(-2){C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+(-3){C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+2{C}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 4 & -6\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $2$nd row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 4 & -6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(-6-5) \\\\  & =-22.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186357515 :question/problem "Calculate the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left of ${a}_{44}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*"] [17592186357515 :question/worked-solution "Using elementary column operations, let's make entries to the left of ${a}_{44}=1$ equal to zero.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+2{C}_{4} \\\\ {C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+{C}_{4} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+(-3){C}_{4}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 3 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $4$th row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 3 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}7 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(49-25) \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186357645 :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the differential equation to the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nSo, we divide both sides of the given equation by ${x}^{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}y={x}^{2}{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe notice that the forcing term $f(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{x}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions:\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}({x}^{3})^{′}-{\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}^{′}{x}^{3} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right){x}^{3} \\\\  & =3x+x \\\\  & =4x\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution of the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={y}_{1}(x)u(x)+{y}_{2}(x)v(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{4}\\int {x}^{4}{e}^{x}\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{4}({x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}-24x+24){e}^{x}, \\\\ v(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\int {e}^{x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={y}_{1}(x)u(x)+{y}_{2}(x)v(x) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)(-\\frac{1}{4}({x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}-24x+24){e}^{x})+({x}^{3})(\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}+{x}^{2}-3x+6-\\frac{6}{x}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}){e}^{x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-3x+6-\\frac{6}{x}){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186357689 :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the differential equation to the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nSo, we divide both sides of the given equation by ${x}^{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{6}{x}{y}^{′}+\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}y=-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe notice that the forcing term $f(x)=-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions:\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}^{′}-{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}^{′}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\left(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}\\right)\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{6}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution of the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={y}_{1}(x)u(x)+{y}_{2}(x)v(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=({x}^{2}-2x+2){e}^{x}, \\\\ v(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\int x{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=(1-x){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={y}_{1}(x)u(x)+{y}_{2}(x)v(x) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)({x}^{2}-2x+2){e}^{x}+\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)(1-x){e}^{x} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{x}){e}^{x} \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186360499 :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$” is true. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =1,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite” is true, while statement “$A$ is indefinite” is false. Since all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$”; “${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite” only."] [17592186360500 :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “${a}_{11}=-3$ and $det(A)=2$” is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-3$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}=2.\n$$\n\n- Statements “${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite” and “$A$ is negative-definite” are true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-3<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}=2>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${a}_{11}=-3$ and $det(A)=2$”; “${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite”; “$A$ is negative-definite”."] [17592186360501 :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “${a}_{11}=6$ and $det(A)=17$” is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=6$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}=17.\n$$\n\n- Statement “${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite” is true, while statement “$A$ is negative-definite” is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=6>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}=17>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${a}_{11}=6$ and $det(A)=17$”; “${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite” only."] [17592186360502 :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “${a}_{11}=-5$ and $det(A)>0$” is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-5$ but \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-8<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements “${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite” and “$A$ is negative-definite” are true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-5<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}=1>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-8<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite”; “$A$ is negative-definite” only."] [17592186360503 :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “${a}_{11}=8$ and $det(A)<0$” is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=8$ but \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}8 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}8 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =21>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite” is true, while statement “$A$ is negative-definite” is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=8>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =\\begin{vmatrix}8 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=7>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=21>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite” only."] [17592186360504 :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “${a}_{11}=-6$ and $det(A)<0$” is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-6$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-6<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite” is false, while statement “$A$ is negative-definite” is true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-6<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix}=2>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-6<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${a}_{11}=-6$ and $det(A)<0$”; “$A$ is negative-definite” only."] [17592186362280 :question/worked-solution "The cross-product rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{3}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=c\\times {\\int }_{2}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the cross-product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{3}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =c\\times {\\int }_{2}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(i-2\\,j+2\\,k)\\times (6\\,i-4\\,j+3\\,k) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(-6+8)i-(3-12)j+(-4+12)k \\\\  & =2\\,i+9\\,j+8\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186362307 :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ and $‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({cos}^{3}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{3}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =⟨-3{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right),\\,3{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right),3{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}⟨-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)‖ & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t-3{cos}^{3}⁡t,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t-3{sin}^{3}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right){sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-3{cos}^{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right),\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right){cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-3{sin}^{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right){\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}-3{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{3},6\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right){\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}-3{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}⟨1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ and ${r}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\times {r}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\left(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)⟨-1,\\,1,\\,0⟩\\times \\left(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)}^{2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\,-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}\\,-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{8}\\cdot 2\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{4}\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\times {r}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)‖ & =\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\frac{{‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\times {r}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)‖}=\\frac{{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{3}}{\\,\\frac{9}{4}\\,}=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186362308 :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t),$ we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨{e}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ),\\,{e}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi )⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{\\pi },\\,-{e}^{\\pi }⟩ \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }⟨-1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & ={e}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =⟨-2{e}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,2{e}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }⟨0,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ and ${r}^{″}(\\pi )$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & ={e}^{\\pi }⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\times {e}^{\\pi }⟨0,\\,-2,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & ={({e}^{\\pi })}^{2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ \\,0 & -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\pi }\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ \\,0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =2{e}^{2\\pi }\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖ & =2{e}^{2\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi ){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖}=\\frac{{(\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\pi })}^{3}}{\\,2{e}^{2\\pi }\\,}=\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186362317 :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(4)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(4)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\sqrt{t-3}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t-3}},1⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(4) & =⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(4)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t-3}}\\right),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(t-3)^{3}}},0⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(4) & =⟨-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(4)$ and ${r}^{″}(4)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(4)\\times {r}^{″}(4) & =⟨2,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(4)\\times {r}^{″}(4)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the radius of the osculating circle at  $t=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(4) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(4){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(4)\\times {r}^{″}(4)‖}=\\frac{(\\sqrt{5})^{3}}{\\,1\\,}=5\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186362340 :question/worked-solution "First, let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & k \\\\ 1 & k & -k \\\\ 5 & k & k\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}k & -k \\\\ k & k\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -k \\\\ 5 & k\\end{vmatrix}+k\\begin{vmatrix}1 & k \\\\ 5 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =({k}^{2}+{k}^{2})+(k+5k)+k(k-5k) \\\\  & =2{k}^{2}+6k+k\\cdot (-4k) \\\\  & =2{k}^{2}+6k-4{k}^{2} \\\\  & =-2{k}^{2}+6k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix is singular, the determinant is $0,$ and we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{k}^{2}+6k & =0 \\\\ k(-2k+6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $k=0$ and $k=3.$"] [17592186362352 :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}⟨0,{e}^{t},1⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}⟨0,1,-{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{2t}+1}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -{e}^{t}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{2t}+1}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{t} & 1 \\\\ 1 & -{e}^{t}\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{t}\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & {e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{2t}+1}⟨-{e}^{2t}-1,-(0-0),0-0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{2t}+1}⟨-({e}^{2t}+1),0,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186362365 :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =T(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\times N(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}⟨3,5,4⟩\\times \\frac{1}{5}⟨4,0,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25\\sqrt{2}}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25\\sqrt{2}}⟨(-15-0),-(-9-16),(0-20)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25\\sqrt{2}}⟨-15,25,-20⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}⟨-3,5,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186362368 :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(1) & =T(1)\\times N(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,2,1⟩\\times \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{5}}⟨-4,5,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 5 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9\\sqrt{5}}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9\\sqrt{5}}⟨(-4-5),-(-4-(-4)),(10-(-8))⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9\\sqrt{5}}⟨-9,0,18⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-1,0,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186362379 :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & n & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-n\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4-n\\cdot 2+0 \\\\  & =4-2n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $-6,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-2n & =-6 \\\\ -2n & =-10 \\\\ n & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186362381 :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2{e}^{2t}}{\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1}}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1}}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}},\\,-\\frac{8{e}^{4t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}}⟨1,\\,-2{e}^{2t},\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+{(-2{e}^{2t})}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{4{e}^{4t}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}}⟨1,-2{e}^{2t},0⟩}{\\left(\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{4{e}^{4t}+1}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1}}⟨1,\\,-2{e}^{2t},\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1}}⟨2{e}^{2t},1,0⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1}}⟨1,\\,-2{e}^{2t},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4t}+1}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2{e}^{2t} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2{e}^{2t} & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4t}+1}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2{e}^{2t} & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}2{e}^{2t} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}2{e}^{2t} & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2{e}^{2t}\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4t}+1}⟨0+0,-(0-0),-4{e}^{4t}-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4t}+1}⟨0,0,-(4{e}^{4t}+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186362389 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & k & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =2\\begin{vmatrix}k & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}4 & k \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(k-2)-3(4-6)+1(4-3k) \\\\  & =2k-4+6+4-3k \\\\  & =6-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{12} & =(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(4-6) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{21}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6-k}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{6-k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{21}^{′}=\\frac{2}{5}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{6-k} & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 2\\cdot 5 & =2(6-k) \\\\ 10 & =12-2k \\\\ 2k & =2 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186362402 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ k & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}k & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}k & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-2(2k-0)+2(k-0) \\\\  & =-4k+2k \\\\  & =-2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(4-2) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2k}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{12}^{′}=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{k} & =-1 \\\\ k & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186364114 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 11 & 0 & -9 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186364115 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -8 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 11 & 3 & -9 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186364116 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 11 & 3 & 0 & -9\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186364117 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -8 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 11 & 3 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186364118 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -8 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 11 & 3 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186364948 :question/problem "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186364948 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =7\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =7(6-1)-1(0+3)-3(0+9) \\\\  & =7\\cdot 5-1\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =35-3-27 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186365547 :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(B) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & k+2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\begin{vmatrix}k+2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 6\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & k+2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(6\\cdot (k+2)-(-5)(-1))+2(1\\cdot (-1)-4\\cdot (k+2)) \\\\  & =3(6k+12-5)+2(-1-4k-8) \\\\  & =3(6k+7)+2(-4k-9) \\\\  & =18k+21-8k-18 \\\\  & =10k+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $70,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10k+3 & =70 \\\\ 10k & =67 \\\\ k & =\\frac{67}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186365550 :question/worked-solution "First, let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}x+2 & 2x & 4 \\\\ 3 & x & x+1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & =(x+2)\\begin{vmatrix}x & x+1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-2x\\begin{vmatrix}3 & x+1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+4\\begin{vmatrix}3 & x \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(x+2)(3x)-2x(8-x)+4(-x) \\\\  & =3x(x+2)-2x(8-x)-4x \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+6x-16x+2{x}^{2}-4x \\\\  & =5{x}^{2}-14x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix is singular, the determinant is $0,$ and we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}-14x & =0 \\\\ x(5x-14) & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\,\\text{or}\\,x=\\frac{14}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{14}{5}.$"] [17592186365555 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-3)(-1)-(2)(2))i-((1)(-1)-(2)(4))j+((1)(2)-(-3)(4))k \\\\  & =(3-4)i-(-1-8)j+(2+12)k \\\\  & =-1i+9j+14k \\\\  & =⟨-1,9,14⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186365811 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=8x{e}^{x}.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})({e}^{x})^{′}-({e}^{-x})^{′}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})({e}^{x})-(-{e}^{-x})({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{8x{e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{x}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4\\int x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(2x-1){e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{8x{e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{-x}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =-(2x-1){e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{-x}+2{x}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & =-(2x-1){e}^{x}+2{x}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & =(2{x}^{2}-2x+1){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{x}+(2{x}^{2}-2x+1){e}^{x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+(B+1){e}^{x}+(2{x}^{2}-2x){e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we redefine the arbitrary constant $B$ as\n\n$$\nB:=(B+1)\n$$\n\nour general solution becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{x}+\\underbrace{(2{x}^{2}-2x){e}^{x}}_{g(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=(2{x}^{2}-2x){e}^{x}.\n$$"]}
